4571	REG3651	Energy Reorganization Act of 1974, as amended	Environmental Health and Safety	42 U.S.C. § 5801 et seq.	3652	Governs the handling, storage, and disposal of radioactive materials. See the General Statement of Policy and Procedures for NRC Enforcement Actions\n\nAdditional Information:\nPrimary objective is to provide adequate protection against the risks to life and property inherent in the transportation of hazardous material in commerce by improving the regulatory and enforcement authority of the Secretary of Transportation.\r\n\r\n \tTraining is required 90 days after employment or change in job function, followed by recurrent training once every three years.\n\nAdditional Information:\nOSHA requires the use of Personal Protective Equipment (PPE) to reduce employee exposure to hazards when engineering and administrative controls are not feasible or effective in reducing these exposures to acceptable levels.\r\nThe regulations clarify what type of PPE employers must provide at no cost to workers and when employers are required to pay for PPE.\n\nAdditional Information:\nApplies to exposure to lead during construction only.\r\nThe university must conduct lead exposure assessments for construction jobs (alternation, repair, demolition, painting, decorating) with possible exposure to lead to determine if exposure threshold met (i.e. &gt; 50 micrograms concentration per cubic meter of air averaged over an 8 hours).\r\nIf threshold met, the university must notify affected employee, institute engineering and work practice controls to reduce exposure to permissible levels, and conduct repeat monitoring. Also applies to contractors.\n\nAdditional Information:\nApplies to occupational exposure to lead by employees in non-construction setting.\r\nThe university must ensure no employee is exposed to &gt; 50 micrograms lead concentration per cubic meter of air averaged over an 8 hours.\r\nWhere there is potential exposure, the university must make a determination as to whether employees are so exposed, and if so must notify the employee, institute corrective action via engineering and work practice controls, and repeat monitoring activities.\n\nAdditional Information:\nBefore cutting/welding can be permitted, the area must be inspected by the individual responsible for authorizing cutting/welding operations, and who must designate precautions to be followed in granting authorization, preferably in the form of a written permit.\r\nCutting/welding shall be permitted only in areas that are or have been made fire safe, and appropriate PPE and clothing based on the type of work performed must be used. (Note: Per OSHA, to minimize carbon monoxide hazard, maintain 3-4 feet of clear ventilation space around generators).	All licensees possessing Category 1 and 2 sources are responsible for reporting information to the National Source Tracking System.\n\nAdditional Requirements:\nUniversity personnel who transport or cause to be transported hazardous materials must file annually a registration statement by June 30.\n\nAdditional Requirements:\nNo specific requirements provided\n\nAdditional Requirements:\nNo specific requirements provided\n\nAdditional Requirements:\nNo specific requirements provided\n\nAdditional Requirements:\nNo specific requirements provided	Campus Safety	federal	t	\N	\N	2025-03-06 14:30:15.612	\N	\N	\N	All licensees possessing Category 1 and 2 sources are responsible for reporting information to the National Source Tracking System.	https://www.ed.gov	Department of Education							All licensees possessing Category 1 and 2 sources are responsible for reporting information to the National Source Tracking System.		\N	\N			\N	\N	1	\N	2025-03-07 15:21:09.709673	\N	t	\N	\N	\N	[{"type": "attestation", "status": "pending", "enabled": true, "required": false}, {"type": "website_publish", "status": "pending", "enabled": true, "required": false}, {"type": "community_communication", "status": "pending", "enabled": true, "requi